A long block collapses past 15 lines, as Carbon's CodeSnippet does. Find in page still finds the hidden lines, and opens the block.

```svelte
<script>
  import { Button, Modal, TextInput } from "carbon-components-svelte";

  let open = false;
  let name = "";

  function submit() {
    open = false;
    name = "";
  }
</script>

<Button on:click={() => (open = true)}>Rename project</Button>

<Modal
  bind:open
  modalHeading="Rename project"
  primaryButtonText="Rename"
  primaryButtonDisabled={!name}
  secondaryButtonText="Cancel"
  on:click:button--secondary={() => (open = false)}
  on:submit={submit}
>
  <TextInput labelText="Project name" bind:value={name} />
</Modal>
```

Soft wrap for long lines: `wrap`.

```js wrap
export const description = "Combo boxes pair a text input with a list of options, so people can filter a long list by typing or pick from it directly.";
```

---

Above is a thematic break.

## Code folds, diffs, and synced tabs

Boilerplate folds away, `collapse={2-9}`, so the lines that matter come first. The fold opens without JavaScript in plain output, and copy takes everything.

```svelte collapse={2-9} {14}
<script>
  import { ComboBox } from "carbon-components-svelte";

  const regions = [
    { id: "us-south", text: "Dallas" },
    { id: "us-east", text: "Washington DC" },
    { id: "eu-de", text: "Frankfurt" },
    { id: "jp-tok", text: "Tokyo" },
  ];
</script>

<ComboBox
  labelText="Region"
  shouldFilterItem={(item, value) => item.text.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase())}
  items={regions}
/>
```

A diff, as `git diff` prints it: ` ```diff-svelte `. The `+` and `-` columns become added and removed lines, and the rest is highlighted as Svelte.

```diff-svelte
@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@
 <script>
   import { ComboBox } from "carbon-components-svelte";
-  let selectedIndex = 0;
+  let selectedId = "us-south";
 </script>
-<ComboBox {items} bind:selectedIndex />
+<ComboBox {items} bind:selectedId />
```

Tabs that sync: every `::: code-group group="svelte"` on the page (and the next visit) shows the version you picked.

::: code-group group="svelte"

```svelte [Svelte 5]
<Button onclick={() => count++}>Clicked {count} times</Button>
```

```svelte [Svelte 4]
<Button on:click={() => count++}>Clicked {count} times</Button>
```

:::

The same group, further down: pick "Svelte 4" above and this one follows.

::: code-group group="svelte"

```svelte [Svelte 5]
<script>
  let { open = $bindable(false) } = $props();
</script>

<Modal bind:open modalHeading="Rename project" />
```

```svelte [Svelte 4]
<script>
  export let open = false;
</script>

<Modal bind:open modalHeading="Rename project" />
```

:::

## Code status, themes, and output

Every header gets an icon: the file's, from its name (`App.svelte`, `package.json`), else the language's. Carbon icons by default; a site can add its own.

A status, from `kind="error"`, `warning`, `success`, or `info`, with an optional `label`:

```js title="legacy.js" kind="warning" label="Deprecated"
import { Search } from "carbon-components-svelte/src/Search";
```

A status with no file name: the language and the status.

```js kind="warning" label="Deprecated"
import Search from "carbon-components-svelte/src/Search/Search.svelte";
```

An icon and no name, `iconOnly`: the name stays for screen readers and as a tooltip.

```svelte iconOnly
<Tag type="green">Passing</Tag>
```

With a status too:

```sh iconOnly kind="error" label="Fails on Windows"
rm -rf node_modules && bun install
```

`dont` and `do` label the pair, and `::: compare` sets them side by side when there's room. A `dont` block has no copy button.

::: compare

```svelte dont
<Button on:click={save}><Save /></Button>
```

```svelte do
<Button icon={Save} iconDescription="Save" on:click={save} />
```

:::

A block in its own theme, `theme="g100"`. Terminal sessions follow Settings › Code › Terminal theme.

```sh theme="g100"
$ bun run build
vite v7.1.0 building for production...
✓ 412 modules transformed.
```

Compact type and spacing for one block, `variant="compact"`; Settings › Code › Code size does it for every block.

```js variant="compact" title="ports.js"
export const ports = { dev: 5173, preview: 4173 };
```

Test output in its ANSI colors, mapped to theme tokens:

```sh
$ bun test
[1mtests/markdown.test.ts:[0m
[32m✓[0m renders headings [2m[0.42ms][0m
[32m✓[0m renders lists [2m[0.18ms][0m
[31m✗[0m renders tables [2m[1.03ms][0m
  [31mExpected:[0m [32m"<table>"[0m
  [31mReceived:[0m [31m"<p>"[0m

 [32m2 pass[0m
 [31m1 fail[0m
```

An `ansi` block takes the escapes as typed (`\e[31m`), and adapts 256-color and truecolor values to the theme: the hue stays, the lightness changes.

```ansi
\e[1mBuild summary\e[0m
\e[38;5;208m⚠ 2 warnings\e[0m  \e[38;2;255;221;0mbundle 412 kB (limit 400 kB)\e[0m
\e[38;2;120;200;255mℹ info\e[0m  \e[38;5;141mtree-shaken 38 modules\e[0m  \e[48;2;0;120;60m\e[97m PASS \e[0m
```

